{
  "id": "the-wrong-pool",
  "name": "The Wrong Pool",
  "category": "services",
  "difficulty": 3,
  "xp": 300,
  "level": 7,
  "description": "The client gets an address. It still cannot reach anything.",
  "brief": "🚨 NEW MACHINE, NO NETWORK\n\nPC-02 asked for an address and got one. 'ipconfig' shows a perfectly well-formed configuration. It still cannot reach a single thing — not even PC-01, sitting on the same switch.\n\nAn address is only useful if it belongs to the network the machine is actually plugged into. Compare what PC-02 was given with what PC-01 has.",
  "hints": [
    "Run 'ipconfig' on both PC-01 and PC-02 and compare them side by side.",
    "Are those two addresses on the same network?",
    "Select DHCP-01 and look at the range its pool hands out.",
    "Fix the pool to cover 10.0.1.100-110 with gateway 10.0.1.1, then renew."
  ],
  "explanation": "The lease applied exactly as configured — the fault was in the pool, not the protocol. Because the address came from a completely different network, PC-02 believed every one of its neighbours was remote, tried to send everything to a gateway it could not reach, and got nowhere.\n\nA misconfigured DHCP pool is a particularly nasty fault because the client looks correctly configured. Only comparing it against a working machine on the same segment gives it away.",
